speechtext:
In view of the very pleasant way in which my hon. friend puts his request, I find it difficult not to accede. I should like to tell the committee how it came about that this proposed amendment was inserted. I am told that there are instances where a loss of $4,000 per day has been incurred which could have been saved merely by attaching a coupling to a ship which was ready to unload. I am told that the Sabbath was made for man and I believe that if such a situation were a personal one to any hon. member of the committee he would be against incurring a loss of $4,000 when it could be saved by attaching a coupling to a pipe line. I can understand this still less when I recall-I am sure the former Minister of Justice will forgive my saying this -that down in his very city on a Sunday afternoon you will find a gang of twenty hired ball players occupying the whole block; that is all right, but silently to attach a coupling to a ship-it cannot be done. Having said this much, I would just like to say, if it is a matter of principle with the hon. member and with anyone else in the house, and if they understood the situation as I have tried to put it in but a few words, that this money will be lost, that a distinguished counsel of Toronto came to Ottawa to see me and said: You do not know what this means in cost to us which is passed on to the consumer, because we have to take care of it. Having stated the facts shortly, I am agreeable to amending the bill.
By the way, let me say that the Lord's Day Act, chapter 123 of the revised statutes of Canada, takes care of this whole matter in railway transportation. If it were the unloading of a train, there would be no trouble: there would be no application to Ottawa for what is sought in this amending bill, but the reason for it is that, the unloading of ships has not advanced as fast as train dispatching or unloading. Again I put it to anyone who is reallv troubled about this: I have personal knowledge of the facts as I have tried to relate them, but I am quite willing to amend the bill so that section 2 will read as follows:
2. Subsection 2 of section 8 of the said act, is amended by adding thereto the following proviso:
"Provided that the collector or other proper officer at the port at which entry of the goods
is to be made may give permission in writing for the lightening of a vessel and unlading of goods,-
(a)	on a statutory holiday other than a Sunday;
(b)	after sunset and before sunrise;
(c)	at a place other than a port;
but such unlading shall be done only in the presence of an officer detailed for such service and under such conditions and upon such terms as the minister may authorize or prescribe.
If that is acceptable, I will ask the Solicitor General to move it.
